Grade 1 thumb ulnar collateral ligament sprain from handheld object in palm catching thumb
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal ulnar MCP joint tenderness, Pain on valgus stress with a firm endpoint, Forceful radial abduction mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-010
Reason for Visit: Acute thumb pain following forceful hyperextension, requiring evaluation to exclude complete ligamentous rupture (Skier's thumb) or avulsion fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute mechanism causing painful loss of pinch grip strength. Urgent assessment was required to diagnose potential complete UCL rupture, which requires immediate strict immobilization to prevent long-term functional impairment and the need for surgical repair, before daytime clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My thumb got pulled backward forcefully and now it hurts sharply whenever I try to pinch anything.
History of Present Illness: The injury resulted from a forceful radial deviation and hyperextension of the thumb metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joint after catching a handheld object. The patient reports focal tenderness strictly localized over the ulnar aspect of the thumb MCP joint. Mild localized swelling and minor ecchymosis have developed along the ulnar border of the thumb base. Pain is severely exacerbated by pinching or grasping objects tightly between the thumb and index finger.
Functional Status: Difficulty executing pincer-grasp tasks; independent in gross motor ADLs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Reduced dominant hand dexterity mildly limits complex functional tasks.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/78 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 84 Kg
General: Alert, protecting the affected thumb, visibly modifying hand movements to avoid MCP loading.
Physical Exam: Hand examination reveals focal ulnar MCP joint tenderness, mild swelling, and early ecchymosis. Active range of motion of the thumb interphalangeal and carpometacarpal joints is intact. Valgus stress testing of the thumb MCP joint in full extension and 30 degrees of flexion elicits pain, but importantly demonstrates a firm endpoint with less than 15 degrees of laxity compared to the contralateral uninjured thumb. Complete absence of a palpable mass (Stener lesion) proximal to the MCP joint. Normal radial sensory nerve function over the dorsal thumb.
Environmental Observations: No immediate safety hazards in the living space.
Medication Reconciliation: No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 thumb ulnar collateral ligament sprain - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Complete UCL rupture (Grade 3/Skier's thumb) - Ruled out due to: Firm endpoint on valgus stress testing without gross joint laxity, and absence of a palpable Stener lesion.

Proximal phalanx avulsion fracture - Ruled out due to: Preserved joint stability and primary tenderness strictly isolated to the ligamentous mid-substance rather than bony insertions.

Radial collateral ligament sprain - Ruled out due to: Complete absence of pain and tenderness on the radial aspect of the MCP joint.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

A high-velocity radial abduction injury to the thumb necessitates immediate exclusion of a complete UCL tear, which carries severe long-term functional implications for hand grip. On-site dynamic stress testing confirmed isolated Grade 1 ligamentous strain with an intact structural endpoint. Urgent after-hours assessment was definitively required to rule out a complete tear requiring surgical referral or rigid thermoplastic splinting, thereby ensuring safe, functional immobilization could be applied immediately to prevent further damage overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed to avoid forceful pincer grips. Applied a supportive thumb spica bandage/splint. Anteroposterior and lateral thumb radiographs are not acutely required unless symptoms fail to improve.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wear the thumb spica splint continuously to isolate the MCP joint. Preventing lateral stress on the healing ligament directly avoids fibrous elongation, ensuring the return of a strong, stable pinch grip.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Thumb spica splint.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days for functional reassessment and to clear return to heavy manual tasks.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department or urgent care if the thumb suddenly becomes completely floppy, if a painful hard lump appears at the base of the thumb, or if severe swelling tracks up the forearm.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Ligamentous injuries of the thumb are prone to recurrent microtrauma if not adequately rested during the initial healing phase.
Clinical Course: Pain with heavy pinching will persist for 2-4 weeks. Full ligamentous strength is typically restored by 6 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate care if your thumb feels entirely disconnected or floppy at the base joint, or if the pain becomes unbearable.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A thumb spica restricts side-to-side movement, protecting the ulnar collateral ligament from stretching while scar tissue forms.
Actionable Steps: Wear the thumb brace at all times during the day. Do not squeeze or pinch heavy objects. Use your opposite hand for opening jars or twisting lids.

